Warlord's Lore
The Truth-Teller
The Truth-Teller speaks only lies, yet every word she utters becomes reality. Born in the moment when the Paradox Faction first gazed upon the Knot of Eternity, she embodies the fundamental contradiction that truth and falsehood are meaningless distinctions. Her weapon, The Honest Lie, cuts through the fabric of certainty itself—each strike doesn't merely wound the body, but wounds the very concept that the target ever existed unchanged.
She claims to have never been born, yet stands before her enemies as proof of her own non-existence. Her followers worship her prophecies, knowing full well that she promises only impossibilities. The greatest paradox of all is that in a universe where she makes lies true, the greatest lie she could tell would be the truth—and thus the truth becomes her most powerful weapon.

The Quantum Empress
The Quantum Empress rules over an empire that exists yesterday, tomorrow, and never. Her coronation occurred in the past tense future, when she simultaneously abdicated her throne to become queen. The crown upon her head was forged from moments that didn't happen, set with jewels of crystallized time that refused to flow.
Her subjects remember her reign before it began, serving a monarch who commands through having never given orders. In battle, her enemies face the terrible certainty of her inevitable victory, even as she leads armies that were defeated before the war started. She is the sovereign of contradictions, the empress of paradox, and her kingdom encompasses every moment that could have been, should have been, and absolutely was not.
In the eons before the current war, whispers speak of a desperate alliance between the Paradox and Lightborne factions. When The Starchild sought to create the ultimate weapon against the Void, it was the Quantum Empress who provided the impossible technology—architects of reality working alongside children of light to forge a construct that existed outside time and space. The Attack Moon bears the paradoxical fingerprints of both civilizations, a weapon both created and never made.

The False Oracle
The False Oracle preaches the gospel of maybe, spreading the holy word that nothing is certain except uncertainty. Her scriptures are written in disappearing ink on pages that exist only when not observed, containing prophecies that come true precisely because they were false.
Before her fall to paradox, she walked among the Lightborne as a minor prophet, interpreting the harmonies of the Eternal Core alongside The Starchild's inner circle. Her gift was to see the spaces between certainties, the shadows cast by absolute light. But when she began speaking of futures that contradicted the Core's supposed perfection, she was cast out as a heretic.
She gathered her first disciples by promising them exactly what would never come to pass—and delivered everything she said they wouldn't receive. Her miracles are performed by failing spectacularly, her blessings bestowed through curses that don't work. She is beloved by those she has betrayed and feared by those she has never met.
The jagged scar that runs from her left temple to her jaw tells a different story—a reminder of her encounter with MK during the Battle of Shifting Sands. Minuscule Knight's Void-Breaker Cannon had found its mark, and though she survived where others would have perished, the wound refused to heal properly. It phases in and out of existence like everything else about her, sometimes visible as a cruel reminder, sometimes absent as if it never was.
Her greatest prophecy speaks of an ending that is also a beginning, a victory achieved through perfect defeat, and a truth so false it becomes more real than reality itself—but she always touches the scar when she speaks of futures that might not come to pass, remembering the light she once served and the certainty she chose to abandon.

The Fallen Angel
Once, she was among the most radiant of the Lightborne—a being of pure stellar fire who sang the harmonies of creation alongside The Starchild herself. Her wings were wrought from concentrated starlight, her voice could kindle dying suns, and her devotion to the Eternal Core burned brighter than a thousand dawns.
But when the Paradox Faction first revealed the Knot of Eternity and spoke of realities beyond light and shadow, she listened. In the hidden archives of Yith, she discovered that the Core itself was not eternal—that even stars must die, and that light without darkness was merely blindness. The revelation shattered her faith like glass.
Her fall was not into evil, but into truth. She embraced the Paradox, and her radiance became contradiction—light that cast shadows, wings that bore her into darkness, a voice that sang both hope and despair. The Lightborne named her traitor, but she had become something more dangerous: a living proof that their absolute certainty was itself uncertain.
Yet unlike others who have turned from the light, she bears no hatred for her former kin. When Lightborne and Paradox forces clash, witnesses speak of her weeping tears of liquid starlight as she fights—not from regret, but from the pain of necessary truth. She still loves The Starchild as a sister, still hears the beauty in the Core's songs, but she cannot unsee what she has learned. Her greatest tragedy is that she remains good, fighting those she loves to save them from their own blinding certainty.
Now she leads those who have seen beyond the binary of light and void, showing them that even angels can choose to fall—and in falling, sometimes find a deeper truth than heaven ever offered.

God Engine Lore:
Möbius Walkers
Forged on the spiraling world of Yith, the Möbius Walkers are not built to wage war but to unravel it. Their mirrored bodies twist like endless Möbius strips, inscribed with the Necronomium’s prophecy of “The Knot that Binds Eternity.” 
Unlike the Solar Colossus or Dreadnut, they do not march as titans but fold reality into themselves. 
Soldiers speak of them appearing in battles centuries before their forging, of strikes that erase the very moment they were made, and of machines that return from destruction because they never truly fell.
To face the Möbius Walkers is to fight against the collapse of causality itself.
